{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556555-resources-for-disparate-characters","id":1556555,"title":"Resources for Disparate Characters","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-12-13T07:14:05-08:00","id":1556555,"title":"Resources for Disparate Characters","content":"Really quick question: What sources (mainly non-fiction books, but I would also take fiction books, movies, etc.) any of you recommend to better understand the culture of the different Disparate Crafts? I'm mainly referring here to the Crafts that ended up in the Disparate Alliance of M20. I know that <i>The Book of Secrets</i> has a lot of resources, like the recommended <i>Destiny Disrupted: A History of the World Through Islamic Eyes</i>, which would be useful to understand the Batini and, to a lesser extent, the Taft\u00e2ni, but I was thinking of getting resources more specific to the mystic traditions represented by each Craft. I thought that <i>The Book of Crafts</i> would have something in its final pages, like the Tradition Books of Revised Edition, but found nothing there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28986-agrat","name":"Agrat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28986_1.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T00:26:02-08:00","id":1556592,"title":"","content":"While based on many real world references, the Crafts don't really represent real mystic traditions. They reference many things, but sometimes are quite contradictory with those very things. Add to that that they're still Mages and, in the end, don't really agree all that much more than members of a given Tradition or something.<br /> <br /> So it depends on what you want exactly out of it. The write-up is enough for you to play a member of the Craft that was raised in the US or something, and even to run the Craft as a major faction if you're STing the game and they're not the core of the story. If you want a character that's culturally appropriate, your first stop should be local news of the area, to try to understand their culture in the present, bonus points if you can narrow it to the specific city the character comes from. If you want a character from a Craft-run community, those are usually anachronic and based on a romanticized version of the culture in question, and then you may need both historical sources about the period or periods &quot;represented&quot;, and some insight into <u>native</u> literature about it.<br /> <br /> Getting the cultural and historical part right is more important than getting the mystic traditions right for several reasons. The first being that, as mentioned, the Crafts are actually made up BS just like the Traditions, so you have all you need in the write-ups. The second is that you need a heavy understanding of the cultural basis to understand the mystic traditions of a real people, or you'll end up with a half-assed hybrid pseudo-philosophy like many the US and Europe generated in the XIX and XX centuries. And the third is that to really understand the pieces the writers used to make the Crafts you have to read <b>A LOT</b>, as frequently they're references to a lot of things at the same time, like an elaborate joke.<br /> <br /> Your best option may be to wikipedia what you want for a broader picture and then use the sources there to zoom in in the aspects that are more important to whatever you'll do. We can help here more if you ask more specific questions about any of them and what you're planning to do with the info, so we can point to the most appropriate source.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T07:59:38-08:00","id":1556608,"title":"","content":"The Traditions and the Conventions represent pop culture stereotypes about magic and mages. The Crafts are simply more stereotypes about the same things. <br /> <br /> If the game was even sneaking up close to realism (humorous thought for a game on this subject) there would be several hundred crafts most with a scattered handful of Mages and a couple of dozen sorcerers. <br /> <br /> Realistic Traditions, Conventions and Crafts would likely be wildly cumbersome to play and get Onyx Path sued.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T18:11:40-08:00","id":1556683,"title":"","content":"As far as the Bata'a go, I can at least offer up Zora Neale Hurston's <b>Tell My Horse: Voodoo and Life in Haiti and Jamaica</b>. Hurston was a writer from the Harlem Renaissance, and the book is a firsthand account of her travels to the Caribbean during the 1930s, including taking part in various voudun and obeah ceremonies as an initiate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-16T17:39:48-08:00","id":1556735,"title":"","content":"Also for the Bata'a, <b>The Serpent and The Rainbow</b> is a nonfiction book by Wade Davis, an ethnobotanist who went to Haiti in the early 80s to investigate recorded cases of zombification (especially the theory that it involved the use of biological toxins). He also wanted to understand why zombi were created in the first place, learning everything he could about the Vodou societies of Haiti. All of this took place during the last days of the Duvaliar dictatorship.<br /> It was loosely adapted into a film in 1988, directed by Wes Craven (of Nightmare on Elm Street and Scream fame), fictionalizing the story and pitting the main character against a bokor who is also a high ranking member of the Tonton Macoutes (secret police). While having certain horror elements, it treats Vodou respectfully and shows what a huge role it played in Haitian culture at the time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-16T17:54:16-08:00","id":1556736,"title":"","content":"A film to watch for inspiration on Chinese Mages in general is <b>Chinese Ghost Story 3. </b>\u200b\u200bit's a comedy adventure that becomes other things as it goes along. The main Vampire is a good inspiration for the Wu-kenge, if you use them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-16T19:29:39-08:00","id":1556739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556555-resources-for-disparate-characters?p=1556736#post1556736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main Vampire is a good inspiration for the Wu-kenge, if you use them.</div> </div> </div> </div>For whatever it's worth, &quot;Chinese Vampires&quot; (Jiangshi) is as much a pop culture BS as the western Vampire, with just vague roots in folklore that are actually completely disparate stories. Almost everything about it, to the extent that you can say there's a well defined &quot;it&quot;, is just movies and novels.<br /> <br /> Other than that, the main differences are:<br /> <br /> 1 - They're stiff, because the Chinese have a stronger association of death with <i>rigor mortis</i> than Europeans, for historical reasons. But other &quot;signs of death&quot; that are a staple here may be underplayed. Or not. Jumping is a common but non-required consequence of stiffness;<br /> <br /> 2 - They're blue because portraying morbid or realistic depiction of the dead in media is considered obscene in China and as such is both shunned and heavily regulated, although not strictly illegal in every context. You'll see they also show western vampires in media as white-but-normal instead of grey-pale or green-rot. Blue isn't their image of deathly pale, it's avoidance of deathly pale;<br /> <br /> 3 - They're hold by slips of paper for the same reason western vampires are hold at bay by crosses, it's just local religious imagery and swapping it around isn't offensive.<br /> <br /> That's to say Jiangshi aren't really a heavy cultural thing in China, and it's virtually impossible to depict them in a more disrespectful way than the Chinese already did. Even depicting them as rotten corpses won't do much if you're not trying to smuggle this content to Chinese children, and then it doesn't really matters if it's a Jiangshi or just a skeleton.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-16T20:20:59-08:00","id":1556743,"title":"","content":"For the Wu Lung (and China in general), Jing Liu's <b>Understanding China Through Comics</b>. It's a five-volume collection of graphic novels that give a very readable and easy to understand overview of 5000 years of Chinese history and cultural movements. (Unfortunately, it ends with 1949 and Communist victory in the civil war, as the author is a native of Beijing and any accurate account of the PRC's history would likely get him in trouble.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-18T18:09:45-08:00","id":1556816,"title":"","content":"I don't think the Go Kamisori Gama were part of the Alliance, only being scouted for potential membership, but for anyone interested in them I offer Stephen Turnbull's <b>Ninja: Unmasking the Myth</b>. It looks at both the historical origins of the concept and what they actually did, as well as at the modern myth and how it developed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T11:32:54-08:00","id":1556876,"title":"","content":"Something for the Batini, as well as a lot of the other groups that were part of the Web of Faith, is <b>Sufism: An Introduction to the Mystical Tradition of Islam</b> by Carl W. Ernst. It's a pretty good overview of what the various Sufi traditions are, how diverse and widespread they are, and how they originated and developed over the past millennia and a half.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-23T14:02:25-08:00","id":1556939,"title":"","content":"As far as the Knights Templar go, they have a rather unique set of issues in that 1) they were a real historical group, and 2) they have been the subject of countless real world conspiracy theories for the past few hundred years. They've been tied to everything from the Assassins cult and the Freemasons to the Priory of Sion and the Illuminati. And that doesn't even get into fictional material like <i>The DaVinci Code</i>, <i>National Treasure</i>, or <i>Assassins Creed</i>. As Umberto Eco quipped in his conspiracy-centric novel <i>Foucault's Pendulum</i>, &quot;The Templars have something to do with everything.&quot;<br /> <br /> Regarding their real-world history, probably the most readable is Dan Jones's <b>The Templars: The Rise and Spectacular Fall of God's Holy Warriors</b>. Jones is one of those &quot;popular historians&quot; who writes mainly for a general audience rather than for academics. He was also one of the historical advisors for the History Channel's <b>Knightfall</b>, a fictional drama about the Templars. (While watchable, just be aware that the show is a work of fiction and takes a lot of dramatic liberties.)<br /> <br /> More encyclopedic (literally) is <b>Knights Templar Encyclopedia: The Essential Guide to the People, Places, Events, and Symbols of the Order of the Temple</b> by Karen Ralls. Ralls is a professor at (IIRC) Oxford, specializing in medieval history and art, and spent about 16 years compiling everything for this book.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, most historians, especially medievalists, who talk about the Templars tend to dismiss all of the modern conspiracy material as unworthy of their time. A cynical person might wonder how much of this is because looking at these (mostly false and sometimes ridiculous) myths and how they developed and evolved would require deep dives into time periods far removed from their area of expertise (ie the last 300 years, including odd political movements, various groups trying to tie themselves to long lost historical things to gain an appearance of greater legitimacy, and outright hoaxes/pranks).<br /> <br /> The same cannot be said about the late Michael Haag, a historian who apparently enjoyed looking into that kind of thing (having written books about Dan Brown's very silly novels and King Tut, among other things). His <b>Templars: History and Myth: From Solomon's Temple to the Freemasons</b> covered some of stuff the Templars have been tied to, especially the 19th century Scottish Freemasons.<br /> <br /> Finally, and most weirdly, is <b>Knights Templar: A Secret History</b> by Graeme Davis. This is not a historical work. Rather it is a part of Osprey Publishing's (famous for their guides for various historical military groups and units) Dark Osprey line, a bunch of pseudohistorical guides about dark urban fantasy topics such as the Roman campaigns against Cthulhu cults or the Martian invasions of 1900. In this case, the first half of the book is mostly real history with some asides about Baphomet and the like. The rest, after the Templars were disbanded, tries to take a lot of the conspiracy theories and weave them into a single coherent narrative. If nothing else, it is an interesting bit of idea fodder.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-23T22:35:36-08:00","id":1556948,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556555-resources-for-disparate-characters?p=1556876#post1556876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something for the Batini, as well as a lot of the other groups that were part of the Web of Faith, is <b>Sufism: An Introduction to the Mystical Tradition of Islam</b> by Carl W. Ernst. It's a pretty good overview of what the various Sufi traditions are, how diverse and widespread they are, and how they originated and developed over the past millennia and a half.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is, but it's also important to note that while the Batini are heavily based on Sufism, some of their precepts are actually opposites.<br /> <br /> Overall that goes for all those sources: learn from them, but keep in mind that the Crafts are <u><b>not</b></u> those groups.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1556555-resources-for-disparate-characters?p=1556939#post1556939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as the Knights Templar go, they have a rather unique set of issues in that 1) they were a real historical group, and 2) they have been the subject of countless real world conspiracy theories for the past few hundred years.</div> </div> </div> </div>More than just a few hundred if we consider that most of what they were accused of in their time was already a lot of conspiracy theory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-24T04:46:02-08:00","id":1556958,"title":"","content":"Pragmatically the best path is to identify the archetype the craft is built on and research the traditions surrounding that archetype. Some books do help for multiple archetypes. Take <b>Magic and Mystery in Tibet,</b> no one takes it seriously as ethnography anymore, even New Agers consider the woman who wrote the book a total flake. However, the pop culture view of Tibetan mystics from the 1890s to the present is influenced by this book. So, even if <b>Magic and Mystery in Tibet </b>has only a tangential connection to Tibet, it is a major influence on all Western ideas about Asian magic. That makes it a useful book for this kind of RPG.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}